  "vulnerability_origin": "The xxHash code was imported in 23590f7c534 with a raw memcpy wrapper (XXH_memcpy) and assumes internal invariants like 0<=memsize<16 for XXH32. PHP can deserialize/seed hash state; if memsize is attacker-controlled or corrupted, XXH32_update can compute (16 - memsize) with unsigned underflow, leading to an oversized memcpy and (apparent) overlapping/wild ranges. The later cebdad4b533 unserialize hardening reduced but did not align the bound with XXH32’s 16-byte buffer invariant.",
  "fix_hints": "History indicates issues around (un)serialization of xxHash state (oss-fuzz #29538). The safest fix is to enforce xxHash’s true invariants at the PHP boundary (unserialize/options) and optionally add a defensive guard before calling XXH32_update, rather than relying on the bundled upstream header to never see corrupted state.",
